> Thanks for the thoughtful approach and have fun with the contribution! Don't hesitate to ask any questions here or on our public [Slack](https://<redacted-host>/).

I hava two possible options to implement `deepClone` for base classes of `Function`:
1. Roughly implemet  `deepClone()` method for every base class.
Example:
```
    private static class Func extends AbstractCastToStrFunction {
        ......
             @participant3
             public Function deepClone() {
                    return new Func();
             }
        ......
    }
```
- Pros: Low runtime cost compare to opition 2
- Cons: A large amount of repetitive code, need implement `deepClone()` from every add function in the future.

2. Provide a default `deepClone()` method in interface/abstractClass which Use reflection to eliminate repetitive code.
Example:
```
    @Override
    default Function deepClone() {
        try {
            Class<? extends UnaryFunction> unaryFunctionClass = this.getClass();
            Constructor<? extends UnaryFunction> unaryFunctionClassCons =
                    unaryFunctionClass.getConstructor(Function.class);
            return unaryFunctionClassCons.newInstance(this.getArg().deepClone());
        } catch (NoSuchMethodException | IllegalAccessException |
                 InstantiationException | InvocationTargetException ex) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(ex);
        }
    }
```
- Pros: Effectively eliminate repetitive code(of cource also need override for 'special' function that not conform to this pattern)
- Cons: A slight runtime performance loss due to use of reflection compare option 1. However, I think the performance loss is acceptable because function.deepClone not happen offen In the whole query/sql execution time.
(I think  the reason of making this trade-off is that Java lacks ability of template-meta programming.)

So, I'd perfer option2 to implement. What do you think about the two options? Or have another good idea?
@reporter

Sorry for the late reply. I'd try to go with option 2, but that might be tricky as signatures of function constructors vary a lot. Alternatively, we could add `newInstance` to UnaryFunction`/`BinaryFunction/... and have each class effectively expose its "constructor" (with a unified interface) though this function. Then in `UnaryFunction` the `deepClone()` impl would be simple:
```java
    default void deepClone() {
        return newInstance(getArg().deepClone());
    }
```

